en we looked at active  cast phase eight  and our control t cells shown here in  the black we see that there's up  regulated caspase-8  indicating fast pathway activation this  is compared to  fast deficient t cells shown in the gray  but when we looked at our fast form bbt  cells we see reduce active caspase eight  similar to those in the fast efficient t  cells suggesting that we  are providing a decoy mechanism and  these t cells are experiencing  what reduced fast pathway signaling  so we found that fast form bb improved  the function of the t  cells in vitro but how are we altering  t cell programming  as i mentioned at the beginning of this  talk we know that there are several  metabolic barriers to anti-tumor  immunity  so there's limited nutrients in the  environment as they're being  used by the tumor cells and also t cells  have been shown to have  reduced number of mitochondria as they  are  exposed to the inhibitory to a  microenvironment  several studies have shown that  increased foreign bb singing can  overcome some of these obstacles and  improve metabolism  so form bb stimulated t cells exhibit  higher spare respiratory capacity  which is the reserve energy store for  cells  and this has been linked to long-term  persistence of memory cells  so in these two papers on the left shown  with form bb  card t cells and on the right shown with  1 db agonism via an antibody  we see that there's increased spare  respiratory capacity for the t cells  that have increased volume db signaling  we saw similar results with our fast  form dbt cells  and that they exhibit higher spare  respiratory capacity which was similar  to those publications i just showed you  and also when we looked by microscopy at  the number of mitochondria  we saw increased mitochondrial  biogenesis in the t cells that expressed  fast form bb  relative to our control t cells so they  are generating increasing  numbers of mitochondria and they have  increased metabolism  um pre similar to previous reports so we  are getting a good foam db signal  through this ifp resulting in  uh remodeling of the metabolism of these  t cells  so we're very excited about these  results we next wanted to test them in  our  in vivo mouse models so we first tested  the fast form bb ifp  and our mouse model of leukemia so for  this model we inject  four million tumor cells into the mice  on day zero  by day five the tumor has disseminated  as is shown here in this ivis image  where the  tumor has spread out in the peritoneal  cavity of these mice  so then we give them a million t cells  after treating with cytokine  which is a chemotherapeutic drug which  we also use in our human patients  and then we assessed for survival over  100 day period  so for this study we did not give them  nice il2 injections which is a  supportive cytokine for the t cells so  once again we're pushing the t cells to  do their job in the absence of support  from il2  and we also gave a single t cell  transfer  when we checked for persistence in the  blood we found that our fast form bbt  cells were highly represented  more highly represented in the blood  than our control t cells  supporting that they had enhanced  persistence  and when we looked at the survival curve  we found that our tcr  t cells that did not have the ifp as  shown here in the solid black line  did prolong survival but ultimately did  not rescue the majority of mice  so while we were getting a survival  increase relative to mice that received  chemotherapy only  as shown in the hatched um black line  the dotted black line  we still did not rescue the majority of  mice  however when we uh treated with  fast-forward uvt cells as shown here in  the red we did  see a significant increase in survival  so  by adding in this extra engineering of  adding in this  ifp we were able to get significant  increase in survival  for mice that received these  ifp-positive t-cells  so we were very excited about those  results the next hurdle that we wanted  to overcome was to look if these ifps  could improve t-cell immunotherapy of  solid tumors and the solid tumor that we  focused on is pancreatic  cancer and pancreatic cancer is a cancer  that  desperately needs improved therapies so  in this chart  there are several cancers shown and in  the purple arrows you see the  changes in survival over a 40 year  period  and for several of these cancers there's  been remarkable  increases in survival with new therapies  and new research  as the treatment options are improved  over time  however pancreatic cancer is done at the  very bottom  and there's very little improvement in  the outcomes for  patients with pancreatic cancer some of  the challenges of pancreatic cancer are  that early detection is uncommon  pancreatic cancer tends to spread  quickly and recurrence is likely  so to model pancreatic cancer in mice we  use the kpc genetically engineered mass  model  of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma or  pda  and we use this model as it  recapitulates  the majority of human patient cancers  in that it has mutations in k-ras and  p-53  and this is held under held under the  control of a pancreas-specific cree  so these mice spontaneously and  continuously generate tumors from the  epithelial cells in the pancreas  so with these mutations they're  constantly generating tumors so we'll  never be able to cure these mice  but we would hopefully be able to shift  the curve with an effective therapy  this model has been successfully used to  screen drugs to predict therapeutic  efficacy in patients  so the results that they saw in this  mouse model translated to humans  so another reason that we like to use  this model  and also the kpc model recapitulates  several barriers to t  cell function of human pda including a  suppressive microenvironment  so in previous work mesothelion  is a target antigen that has been shown  to be reliably expressed  in various stages of pda both in human  disease and in the kpc mouse model  however um when t cells  tcrs were generated to mesothelion and  those tcrt cells were used in therapy  we found that the tumor-specific t cells  became rapidly dysfunctional in the  tumor microenvironment  so these data are from day eight post  transfer and if we compare t  cells pulled from the tumor versus t  cells from the spleen  we already see a marked decrease in the  ability of these t cells to produce  effector cytokines  so the way that this model works is that  we  screen these mice by ultrasound to  determine when tumors arise  so in a healthy mouse we see on the left  side we see a nice  normal healthy bright pancreas but on  the right side we can now visualize  tumors by ultrasound  and so these mice would be enrolled to  study and randomly assigned to treatment  groups  so for our initial studies in solid  tumors we enrolled mice to  the study when tumor was detected we and  then  gave them an enrollment dose of 10 to  the seventh each of tcrt cells  and also mesothelioma pulsed splenocytes  as an in-vivo antigen boost for these t  cells  again after cytosine which is that  chemotherapeutic drug  and then we took the mice down at day 28  and analyzed  to see how our t cells were doing and  again this is in the absence of il2  therapy  so when we looked in the tumor by  histology we saw an increased  intra-temporal concentration of  ifp-positive t-cells at day 28  so we saw increased number of t-cells  were able to  infiltrate the tumor  and looking at it in a little bit of a  different way when we did a paired  injection  where we combined controlled t cells  with our  fast formed bt cells at a one-to-one  ratio and injected those into the mice  by day 28 there was a significant  skewing of the fast-forward bbt cells  in the tumor so although they started at  a 50-50 mix  by day 28 it was more of an 80 of the t  cells in the tumor where our vascular  bbt cells relative to about 20 percent  of the tcronic t cells  so we next um started the survival study  so again we enroll those mice to study  when tumor is detected by ultrasound  and then we give them an enrollment dose  of 10 to the 7th t cells and  mesothelioma  spanish size post cytosine and then we  repeat the t  cell apc infusion without cytosine every  two weeks for a total of three infusions  and then assess the mice for survival  so as we've shown previously  immunotherapy with mesothelion targeted  t  cells improved survival from pda  relative to mice that received  chemotherapy only as shown in the dashed  line  the solid black line showed the tcr  controlled t-cell  therapy but what was really exciting for  us was to see the shift of the curve  that we achieved when we  treated with my t-cells engineered to  express the fast-forward bbt  cells so now we see a shift of the curve  of a median survival of 37 days for the  control t  cells to 65 days for our fast foreign  cells  and this was strongly statistically  significant  we bled the mice for t cell persistence  28 days after their final t cell  transfer  and we were unable to detect transfer t  cells and the ones surviving  tcr only control mouse however for all  four of the surviving  fast-forward uv t-cell recipients we  were able to detect transfer t  cells so we saw increased t-cell  persistence  in the mouse and also increased  trafficking as we showed um  by histology and bipolar cytometry we've  also now  taken these studies into the iv8 ovarian  cancer model  and we found similar results where we  get enhanced survival  of mice that receive our fast-forwarding  t cells in the ovarian cancer model  and we've also translated these  experiments with human  uh t cells with human primary t cells  and we've  uh again used the incucite assay with  pancle and tumor cells  and so um in the gray without any t  cells we see that the tumor  cells go out over time um when you add  in  either tcr only t cells or control t  cells with just the mesothelion specific  tcr  or in the blue um a construct that just  has the  the fast active domain so it's a decoy  receptor we see there is  reduced um tumor persistence but really  the big difference is with  the a red curve where you see that the  fast-forward bt cells are able to  eradicate the tumor cells in the dish  so we're very excited about these  results not only for the in  increased survival benefits that we've  seen  but also in that as  we know there are several obstacles that  inhibit the anti-tumor t cell response  that i presented at the very beginning  we now know that ifps have the potential  to overcome  several of these inhibitory barriers so  with our ifp  t cells we've seen enhanced metabolism  as well as reduced or ablated  suppression by decoy  so we've seen reduced endogenous fast  signaling  due to our fast formula expression we've  seen reduced exhaustion of our t cells  they're able to kill round after round  of tumor cells as we've shown in the sco  assay  there's a lower threshold of activation  these t-cells are able to proliferate  with a very low level of stimulation  um and also these t-cells are able to  get into the tumor  and persist in in vivo so we have  increased intra temporal concentration  as well as  persistence in our mouse models  so with that i'd like to thank my lab we  just started on may 1st  and so edison chew is the first member  of my lab i'd also like to thank my  former mentor phil greenberg  and the group of researchers that worked  with me during my time there  including nicola garcia cody jenkins and  katrina polizzikov  and also our collaborators dan riddell  mike jensen and ode chapui  and our sources of funding which include  the leukemia and lymphoma society  juno therapeutics and then we have  several new  projects that we're working on now and  we're always interested in new  collaborations and partnerships  so please feel free to reach out if you  have some ideas you would like to work  with us  thank you for your time
 

